  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfternoon Update: Based on latest high resolution forecast model\nguidances, latest satellite, and radar trends, a few \nthunderstorms this afternoon from the Schoharie Valley, Greater \nCapital Region, the Berkshires, eastern Catskills, Mid-Hudson \nValley, Taconics, and Litchfield Hills can become severe. Taking\na deeper dive into the current environment, there is enough \ningredients such as energy and instability for storms to become \nstrong to severe with the current forecast supporting these \nstorms moving through from west to east. Thunderstorms can \ndevelop through 8 PM tonight as once the sun sets, we lose \nsignificant energy to produce strong to severe storms. Primary \nconcerns with any developed thunderstorm through this afternoon\nare heavy downpours (up to 1 inch in rainfall amounts with \nthem), lightning, and strong to damaging wind gust. Once showers\nand thunderstorms come to an end this evening, a quieter\novernight is in store with humid conditions until the cold \nfront moves through tomorrow bringing relief from the humid \nconditions. \n\nPrevious Discussion: Mid-level shortwave currently tracking \nacross the Great Lakes region will pass through this afternoon, \ninitiating additional scattered to numerous showers and \nthunderstorms. Mid level lapse rates will be slightly steeper \n(6-6.5 C/km) than recent days due to some cooling aloft and \nheight falls spreading into the region. In addition, mid levels \nwill be a bit drier. As instability builds this afternoon with \nHREFs suggesting MU CAPES 1000-2000 J/kg, greatest within and \neast of the Hudson River Valley, forcing from the approaching \nshortwave should allow for scattered showers/thunderstorms to \ndevelop across western areas during the early/mid afternoon, \nbefore tracking eastward through the Hudson River Valley and \nwestern New England late this afternoon through early this \nevening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfternoon Update: No changes. See discussion below for more\ndetails.\n\nIt will remain warm and humid today, though maximum heat indices\nwill be a bit less than the last 2 days, generally 90-95 within\nriver valleys as actual high temps reach 85-90 with dewpoints in\nthe upper 60s to lower 70s. A cold front will move through the\nregion Sunday morning, with decreasing humidity levels expected\nin its wake for Sunday afternoon as dewpoints drop into the 60s\nfor most areas, with even some 50s possible across portions of\nthe SW Adirondacks and Mohawk Valley by late afternoon. So,\ndespite similar maximum temperatures to today (85-90 in valleys\nand upper 70s/lower 80s for higher terrain), max heat indices\nwill be even less, mainly in the upper 80s/lower 90s in valley\nareas. It will also become breezy Sunday afternoon in the wake\nof the front, with west/northwest wind potentially gusting up to\n20-25 mph at times.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAfternoon Update: No changes. See discussion below for more \ndetails.\n\nWeather pattern becomes more active this week as broad upper\nlevel troughing develops across the Great Lakes/Northeast and \nSE Canada. Strengthening mid/upper level winds (H500 winds 30-50\nKT) on the south/southwest periphery of the developing trough \nwill enhance the potential for stronger thunderstorms with any \ndisturbance that passes through, especially should they track \nfarther north allowing for pockets of greater instability to \ndevelop across our region. One disturbance looks to pass through\nMonday afternoon/evening, then again possibly late \nTuesday/early Wednesday and perhaps again toward late week. Will\nhave to watch trends with each system that passes through for \nthe possibility of strong/severe thunderstorms given the strong \nwind fields aloft.",
